00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.429 align:start position:0%
 
well joining us now is deputy leader of  the labour party angela rainer hello to  you ms rainey thanks for joining us i  suppose oh yes you're stuck between a  rock and a hard place this morning  aren't you because you're glad to see  what happened to boris johnson but you  i'm guessing you want him to stay in  post now until an election  no i don't want boris johnson to stay in  post because as you know many of the  public see politicians in general as uh  untrustworthy and i think boris johnson  is pulling down the standards in public  life which is why we've got the motion  today to uphold them but i think he he's  doing a very disservice to the country  and it's pretty clear now that the  british public have lost all confidence  in boyce johnson i mean if you can't  have a prime minister that follows his  own rules and doesn't lie to parliament  and lie to the british public then where  is the standards you know we've lowered  the tone so bad that um you know we  could get away with just about anything  so i don't think it's a good day i think  he is mortally wounded now and i think  that you know he's he's only got an 80  of majority within parliament and  therefore he's pretty clear that over 70  percent of his backbenchers didn't back  him so i think he should have done the  right thing by now and resigned already  but this prime minister doesn't really  consider himself to follow rules but  don't you want a wounded prime minister  in position while you sort your party  out  and no i think you know our party  obviously in 2019 in the general  election lost badly and i think since  myself and care have taken over we've  been making steady progress if you'd  have said we are where we are today  back then i think most people would have  laughed at us but we know we've got work  to do but i think you know when you've  got a prime minister who lies to the  british public who breaks his rules i  mean number 10 was the most heavily  fined building a house in the whole of  the united kingdom when it came to the  covid rules cleaners were having to  clean up vomit and wind off the walls i  mean it wasn't  a one-off this was serial partying going  on in number 10 when people couldn't see  their loved ones i don't think it's  going away i think every day that boris  johnson is prime minister is damaging  the standards in public life and it  damages us all so i don't think it's a  good thing that he continues i think  he's arrogant i think he's dismissive i  think he's a liar and i don't think he  should hold the office of prime minister  yeah but you you as a party are still  not ready to run the country are you  i think we are i think we've shown that  actually when you look at the issues  that people are facing at the moment  whether that's the cost of living the  travel chaos whether that's the  situation with the nhs we've got the  answers to those uh damaging questions  that people are asking at the moment  boris johnson is too busy naval gazing  looking after himself to look at the  issues that many working people as this  country are facing at the moment and  that's damaging for people because you  know while rather than saving and  supporting people that need that help  now  that vote for us boris johnson is trying  to save his own skin  but how can you be ready when the leader  and the deputy leader are still facing a  police inquiry  well because you see here's the  difference with the police inquiry on  that occasion when we were in work we're  very confident that we were working and  we said categorically if we were issued  with a fixed penalty notice we would  resign because standards in public life  matter it might have be on me it might  be on care and it might be on boris  johnson he broke the rules that he set  and he's decided that he those rules  don't apply to him i think it's damaging  for the public because you know if he  breaks those rules and lies to the  public now what else is he going to lie  about you know he's bringing everyone  down i think that's the issue here and  the big difference is that there is one  investigation into one work event that  we're very confident that will be  uh will be fine and if it isn't we've  said categorically that we shouldn't  carry on in post i think the prime  minister over 120 fixed penalty notices  numerous parties going on in number 10  and he's had a fixed penalty notice and  he doesn't think he should resign i  think that's appalling  but facts are facts aren't they and the  leader and the deputy leader of the  opposition have both been received  questionnaires so in other words  question of police caution and they  still have a police investigation  outstanding you're not ready you can't  run the country yet while you're in that  position  well yeah of course we can you know  we're very ready to do that we've been  talking about the issues that matter to  people like the windfall tax on the  energy companies to support people to  look at a renewable energy programme  that creates jobs in this country and  deals with our long-term energy needs to  have a new deal for working people that  provides  flexibility and work that pays for  people we've got a programme for change  and we're getting on with that and we're  very clear that if the police found us  to be guilty and give us a fixed penalty  notice we would resign we're very  confident that we didn't break the rules  and that we followed all the rules to  the letter  well that's rolling the dice isn't it  and that's putting the party the labour  party in jeopardy because if that did  happen and you don't know if it will or  not if it did and you were in the middle  of a general election campaign say the  prime minister calls a snap election  then the leader and the deputy leader  would have to step down what would that  do for the uh credibility of the party  but i don't think it's rolling the dice  i think it's called doing the right  thing i think there has to be a standard  a line that is drawn between the  contract of the british public and those  that hold public office it's an honor  and a privilege to have the roles that  myself and care have got and we know  that we do that um with the  goodwill of our party and with the  people that voted for us as mps we're  saying that if we broke the rules we  would resign we would not carry on boris  johnson says it doesn't matter and  there's laughed in people's faces and  lied to them when they couldn't see  their loved ones now if myself and kid  did resign we're not the only people in  the labour party we're a team we work  together in the labour party and have  done that and we work in the interests  of the public unlike boris johnson who  is a bit of a  tim pop uh dictator now who thinks he  could just carry on regardless anyway i  was broken his own rules and he's not  listening he's lost the confidence of  the british public and he's now lost the  confidence of many of his mps is limping  on because he doesn't think the rules  apply to him and i think he's gonna he's  gonna find out pretty  clear through the by-elections that  actually the british public do not like  liars people who break the rules and i  think it's going to be very damaging for  his party and for politics in general if  the prime minister carries on  timbuktu tin pot dictator that's very  strong uh words there from you this  morning ms rainer will the labour party  be calling for a vote of no confidence  in the whole cup in the whole government  well today we've set out a motion which  is about upholding standards in public  life which boris johnson again wants to  cherry-pick that the chair have been  very clear that the whole report should  be implemented and we agree with that so  we've put motion down today to ensure  that we at least set a bar that is  higher than where boris johnson is  so that we can you know restore some  trust in public office and of course  we'll look at all options going forward  but you know the prime minister ignores  he should have resigned months ago he  knows he broke the law he knows he lied  to the british public but you know he'll  just keep limping on because he doesn't  think the rules apply to him and he's  laughing at the general public and  taking them for fools and i think most  of the public will not move on while  boris johnson is prime minister  and in answer to my question will you be  calling for a vote of no confidence in  the whole government  well we'll see how we go on for the rest  of the week obviously we'll speak to  other  other parties as well but we're very  clear that the prime minister doesn't  have the confidence of the house pretty  clear from the votes of his own mps that  he's lost a lot of significant  confidence within his own political  party and if he had an ounce of  integrity which he removed from the code  he would have resigned by now

